<article class="env-remark" id="08IU">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08IU">Remark <span data-tag="08IU">99.7.3</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme, $X$ an algebraic space over $S$, and $\mathcal{F}$ a quasi-coherent $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. Suppose that $\{ f_ i : X_ i \to X\} _{i \in I}$ is an fpqc covering and for each $i, j \in I$ we are given an fpqc covering $\{ X_{ijk} \to X_ i \times _ X X_ j\} $. In this situation we have a bijection </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \left\{  \begin{matrix}  \text{quotients }\mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{Q}\text{ where } 

\\  \mathcal{Q}\text{ is a quasi-coherent }

\\ \end{matrix} \right\}  \longrightarrow \left\{  \begin{matrix}  \text{families of quotients }f_ i^*\mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{Q}_ i \text{ where } 

\\  \mathcal{Q}_ i\text{ is quasi-coherent and } \mathcal{Q}_ i\text{ and }\mathcal{Q}_ j

\\  \text{ restrict to the same quotient on }X_{ijk} 

\end{matrix} \right\}   \]
</div>
  <p> Namely, let $(f_ i^*\mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{Q}_ i)_{i \in I}$ be an element of the right hand side. Then since $\{ X_{ijk} \to X_ i \times _ X X_ j\} $ is an fpqc covering we see that the pullbacks of $\mathcal{Q}_ i$ and $\mathcal{Q}_ j$ restrict to the same quotient of the pullback of $\mathcal{F}$ to $X_ i \times _ X X_ j$ (by fully faithfulness in Descent on Spaces, Proposition <a href="/tag/04W8" data-tag="04W8">74.4.1</a>). Hence we obtain a descent datum for quasi-coherent modules with respect to $\{ X_ i \to X\} _{i \in I}$. By Descent on Spaces, Proposition <a href="/tag/04W8" data-tag="04W8">74.4.1</a> we find a map of quasi-coherent $\mathcal{O}_ X$-modules $\mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{Q}$ whose restriction to $X_ i$ recovers the given maps $f_ i^*\mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{Q}_ i$. Since the family of morphisms $\{ X_ i \to X\} $ is jointly surjective and flat, for every point $x \in |X|$ there exists an $i$ and a point $x_ i \in |X_ i|$ mapping to $x$. Note that the induced map on local rings $\mathcal{O}_{X, \overline{x}} \to \mathcal{O}_{X_ i, \overline{x_ i}}$ is faithfully flat, see Morphisms of Spaces, Section <a href="/tag/03MK" data-tag="03MK">67.30</a>. Thus we see that $\mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{Q}$ is surjective. </p>
